  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Opioid use disorder, in sustained remission (F11.21)</content>. Sustained remission continues. Naloxone is in place at home and when traveling, and his travel staff know how to use it. [S]<list><item>Draft the written plan for any future pain together, to be finished at the six-month visit: non-opioid treatment first, and if an opioid is ever needed after surgery or an injury, the smallest supply for the shortest time, one prescriber, a support person told, and a follow-up visit within a week.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Blindness of both eyes since childhood (H54.0X55)</content>. Eyes comfortable and quiet. [S]<list><item>Yearly ophthalmology review; same-day care for eye pain or redness.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Fall prevention</content>. No falls; his routine of a spoken orientation to each new room works well. [S]<list><item>Continue the same routine.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Eye comfort and ocular surface check: pain, redness, discharge</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No pain, redness, or discharge</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Tonometry (eye pressure)</td><td>Every glaucoma visit; for blind eyes, only if pain or redness</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Painless blind eyes, as advised by ophthalmology</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Comprehensive eye examination</td><td>Yearly for blind eyes, or with symptoms</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Ophthalmology examination two months ago</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>At intake, at six months, and yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 2 three months ago; mood good; next at six months</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review and safety plan, with the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide or self-harm; the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline still saved in his phone</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Recovery check: any return to use, cravings, triggers, and supports</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No return to use and no cravings; touring and hotel stays named as times to be careful, with a plan to call a friend if cravings come</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Naloxone at home and when traveling</td><td>At intake; check expiry at each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>In date, at home and in his travel bag; travel staff shown how to use it</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Drug-use screen (DAST-10)</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>DAST-10 0 three months ago</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Alcohol screen (AUDIT-C)</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>AUDIT-C 2 three months ago</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hepatitis C, hepatitis B, and HIV testing</td><td>At entry to care for a person with a substance use disorder</td><td>Due</td><td>Planned for the annual wellness visit, as recorded at the four-week visit</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Written plan for any future acute pain or surgery</td><td>Before any planned procedure, and once in remission</td><td>Due</td><td>Drafted today; to be finished at the six-month visit</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Accessible documents and communication</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Visit summaries continue as accessible electronic documents</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medicine labeling in braille or audio</td><td>With every new prescription</td><td>Up to date</td><td>No new prescriptions</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>CDC STEADI fall-risk screen</td><td>Every visit from age 65</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No falls; steady gait</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Advance care planning</td><td>Yearly from age 65</td><td>Due</td><td>At the annual wellness visit</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Visit summaries, results, and medicine information delivered in an accessible electronic format, and medicine labels in braille or audio, by the four-week visit. Outcome by 2026-03-31: Visit summaries sent as accessible documents; the pharmacy provides audio labels and a braille label on the naloxone [S]</item><item>Naloxone at home and in his travel bag, with a tactile label, and the people he travels with shown how to use it, by the three-month visit. Outcome by 2026-06-02: Naloxone in both places; travel staff shown how to use it [S]</item><item>Stay in sustained remission, with no return to opioid use, through six months. In progress; target 2026-09-01 [S]</item><item>No falls through six months. In progress; target 2026-09-01 [S]</item><item>A written plan for any future acute pain or surgery that uses non-opioid treatment first and builds in safeguards if an opioid is ever needed, by the six-month visit. In progress; target 2026-09-01 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Substance Use Disorders (2021); The ASAM National Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Opioid Use Disorder: 2020 Focused Update (2020); Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma Preferred Practice Pattern (2025); Vision Rehabilitation Preferred Practice Pattern (2023); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, glaucoma and depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monographs (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Six-month review with the PHQ-9 and the finished pain plan. Next scheduled visit 2026-09-01: six-month review with depression screening and a plan for any future pain. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
